  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/d51ed648be79858e9dd211494f706c1f8658364e10d14dca8418b69cf4ab99de.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-2$ and $x=4.$ Given that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "We have ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{\u2032}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-2) & -2 & (-2,4) & 4 & (4,\\infty ) \\\\ f & \u2197 & \\text{no extr.} & \u2197 & \\text{max} & \u2198 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032} & + & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{\u2032}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,4).$",
